 名/票券: https://docs.google.com/forms/d/e/1FAIpQLSfgP3t8lQvY7_dQbsN-N-
 2J6dlH37dh9r5X0Ot0QxSxNHGhow/closedform\n\nJoin us for an Artist Salon: No
 rth African Traditions\, New Music Creating a Concerto for Oud and Baroque
  Orchestra\, a free public conversation presented by Philharmonia Baroque 
 Orchestra & Chorale\, Stanford Live\, and Stanford's Department of African
  and African American Studies.\n\nDiscover the story of the oud—the fret
 less\, pear-shaped lute whose sound has shaped musical traditions across N
 orth Africa and the Middle East for centuries and which is an ancestor of 
 the modern guitar.\n\nComposer Tarik O'Regan and oud virtuoso Joseph Tawad
 ros will take audiences behind the scenes of a new piece O’Regan has com
 posed for oud and Baroque orchestra\, scheduled to premiere at Bing Concer
 t Hall on February 7\, 2027.\n\nO'Regan\, whose music has been performed b
 y leading ensembles around the world\, will discuss the inspiration behind
  the work\, while Tawadros—whose performances have introduced internatio
 nal audiences to the expressive sound of the oud—will share the instrume
 nt's rich musical traditions\, demonstrate its remarkable range\, and offe
 r insights into bringing this new composition to life. The conversation wi
 ll be moderated by Stanford professor and acclaimed scholar Professor Matt
 hew D. Morrison\, whose work explores music\, culture\, and identity.\n\nT
 he event also features live musical demonstration by Joseph Tawadros\, a p
 erformance by the Stanford Middle Eastern Ensemble\, and an audience Q&A\,
  offering attendees the opportunity to engage directly with the artists.\n
 \nDate: Tuesday February 
